          { name: "Renter Household Cost-of-Living Concerns", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 25.6% renter rate represents an economically precarious population sensitive to rent inflation, utility costs, and housing affordability—concerns that typically correlate with Democratic opposition in Republican districts. At a $55,038 median income, renters have little financial buffer for cost shocks, and their concerns can shift a district's margin if they mobilize around affordability and economic fairness. This population's needs are distinct from homeowner priorities and deserve specific legislative attention to prevent drift toward opposition candidates." },
          { name: "American Indian / Alaska Native Community Engagement", tag: "careful", why: "The ~6.29% demographic gap almost certainly represents AIAN residents whose tribal governance needs—healthcare access, sovereignty issues, cultural institutions, and tribal economic development—are real constituent concerns. In a 74-26 district, this population is unlikely to shift the overall margin, but neglecting them represents a governing failure and creates vulnerability if tribal community concerns become a mobilizing issue in a future, tighter election cycle." },
          { name: "Treating 74% as a Durable Floor", tag: "avoid", why: "A 74% result is substantial, but it reflects current conditions in a specific election cycle, not a permanent baseline. In a district with $55,038 income, 25.6% renters, and 20.8% college attainment, economic downturns, healthcare cost spikes, or property tax increases can shift household voting behavior rapidly. Assuming the 74% is safe and reducing constituent attention to working-class economic concerns is the fastest path to vulnerability." },
          { name: "Conflating Low Opposition with Strong Constituent Relationships", tag: "avoid", why: "The 26% opposition may reflect low awareness, low turnout among opposition voters, or genuine preference for the incumbent—but it does not necessarily indicate strong constituent relationships with renters, AIAN populations, or younger working-class households. A legislator who assumes 74% means 74% of households are aware of or satisfied with their representation is likely overestimating actual constituent engagement and vulnerable to a better-organized challenger." }
