          { name: "Rural Healthcare Access and Cost", tag: "lean-into", why: "A 41.7-year median age with modest $62,574 income creates a constituency facing rising healthcare costs on relatively fixed earnings. Rural Kansas healthcare access is degrading as small hospitals close and specialist care concentrates in regional centers, creating direct hardship for aging residents. Specific legislative focus on rural hospital viability, telemedicine infrastructure, and healthcare cost transparency addresses the material economic vulnerability that underlies the unopposed result and is the most credible foundation for organized opposition if left unaddressed." },
          { name: "Agricultural Economy and Small-Town Economic Viability", tag: "lean-into", why: "A $62,574 median income in a rural Kansas district reflects economic dependence on farming, small business, and local agriculture-adjacent employment—all sectors facing consolidation and margin pressure. Specific legislative attention to agricultural infrastructure, water rights protection, farm succession planning, and small-business tax fairness creates a governance agenda that speaks to the actual economic foundation of the constituency rather than abstract partisan positioning. This is the most concrete way to maintain the unopposed advantage and prevent emergence of a challenger mobilized around rural economic decline." },
          { name: "Out-Migration of Young Adults and Educational Pathway", tag: "careful", why: "The 19.8% college-educated rate combined with 41.7-year median age suggests a community where young adults with post-secondary credentials have departed for larger cities with more opportunity. Whether this reflects genuine absence of local opportunity or lack of visible pathway to economic mobility locally requires specific assessment. Attention to vocational training, apprenticeship, and small-business support can address real constraints, but misreading the cause of out-migration (as a supply-side education failure rather than demand-side lack of opportunity) will produce ineffective policy." },
          { name: "Treating Unopposed Victory as a Durable Baseline", tag: "avoid", why: "An unopposed win is not evidence of durable partisan dominance or satisfied constituency—it is evidence that no credible opposition coalition has organized. In a district with aging demographics, modest income, and material economic vulnerability, the absence of opposition is a fragile advantage. Any representative who responds to unopposed victory with reduced constituent engagement or who defaults to abstract partisan messaging without addressing healthcare costs, agricultural viability, or property tax burden will find that a credible challenger can mobilize latent discontent." },
          { name: "Assuming Small-Town Kansas Preferences Without Specific Engagement", tag: "avoid", why: "Rural Kansas communities are not interchangeable, and KS-HD-9's specific economic profile—$62,574 income, 19.8% college education, homeowner-dominant, aging—requires governance responses tailored to those numbers, not generic rural conservative positioning. Relying on inherited partisan alignment while ignoring the material economic constraints facing the actual constituency will create an opening for a challenger who offers concrete solutions to healthcare costs, property tax burden, or agricultural economic viability." }
