          "The renter majority (45%) combined with a median age of 35.3 and college attainment of 43.6% suggests an urban or suburban district with a significant proportion of younger professionals, service workers, and credential-holding adults who rent rather than own. At $80,211 median household income—solidly middle-class but not wealthy—this constituency faces concrete pressure points: rental market tightness, student loan burden, healthcare cost exposure, and childcare access are the daily economic realities for a significant portion of the district. The unopposed result likely reflects that Republican recruitment failed to mount a credible challenger in a district where these economic concerns are material and immediate, not abstract. A future challenger who addresses housing affordability, wages in service and professional sectors, and healthcare access with specificity will find an audience. Vaughn's continued hold on this seat depends on demonstrating tangible results for the younger, educated, rent-paying professionals who are actually living here—not on the assumption that an unopposed 2024 victory represents a durable coalition."
