          { name: "Treating 100% as a Stable Democratic Baseline", tag: "avoid", why: "The unopposed 2024 result reflects either Republican strategic retreat or resource constraints, not a permanent Democratic lock on HD-29. College-educated, high-income urban professionals have proven persuadable in recent cycles when Republicans present competent governance alternatives, fiscal responsibility messaging, or quality-of-life platforms tailored to urban concerns. Meyer should assume that Republican recruitment will resume and that a credible Republican challenger could activate 35-40% of the district's voters on fiscal, competence, or localism grounds if Meyer's governing record is perceived as inadequate or if constituent relationships have atrophied." },
          { name: "Assuming Renter Voters Are Economically Marginal or Ideologically Locked Democratic", tag: "avoid", why: "At a $87,656 median income and 60.5% college attainment, HD-29's renter constituency is affluent, educated, and not ideologically monolithic. These voters rent as a deliberate choice reflecting lifestyle preferences or life-stage position, not economic desperation, and they can be persuaded by Republican 'housing-supply' and 'property-rights' messaging if they perceive Democratic policy as regulatory overreach or fiscally reckless. Meyer must engage renters as sophisticated constituents with material interests in housing economics, not as a captive progressive constituency—this is the difference between holding 48.9% of the district and losing a segment of it to Republican recruitment." }
