          { name: "Education & Workforce Pipeline as Economic Gateway", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 15.6% college attainment rate in a young district (median age 31.9) is an extraordinary structural constraint on economic mobility — substantially lower than comparative rural districts and indicative of either limited educational access or limited completion. Community college affordability, vocational training partnerships, and K-12 funding that supports both college prep and direct workforce skills are not secondary education issues; they are the primary lever for opening economic opportunity for a demographic majority whose income prospects are directly constrained by educational access. A representative who makes this visible and measurable is building durable constituent relationship; one who treats it as standard budget line item is missing the district's most urgent need." },
          { name: "Cross-Community Coalition Leadership", tag: "careful", why: "With 35.63% Hispanic/Latino and 35.26% Black/African American populations and no single demographic majority, HD-34 requires a representative who builds sustained, authentic relationships across communities rather than cycling attention based on electoral cycle. Hispanic and Black communities often experience divergent economic and service priorities — immigration status, criminal justice and policing, healthcare access, small business support — and a representative who treats them as a monolithic 'minority' constituency rather than distinct communities with overlapping but separate needs will find both groups underserved and potentially mobilizable by a challenger who promises more specific attention." },
          { name: "Treating Unopposed Victory as Electoral Safety", tag: "avoid", why: "A 2024 unopposed result in a district with $46,637 income, 47.3% renters, and a young, diverse demographic profile should be read as the absence of organized opposition, not as a floor for future electoral performance. Without a challenger, there is no visible track record of the incumbent delivering material economic benefit to constituents; the next cycle will present an opportunity for any credible challenger who can articulate a more specific or more aggressive agenda on housing, wages, or education to contest the seat seriously. This is a district where the incumbent must build durable constituent trust through visible delivery, not rely on the 2024 margin." },
          { name: "Generic Partisan or Values-Based Messaging", tag: "avoid", why: "At $46,637 income with 47.3% renters and 31.9 median age, this constituency is not evaluating representation through the lens of partisan ideology or abstract values — they are evaluating on whether their rent is rising faster than their wages, whether childcare is affordable, and whether educational opportunity is expanding. A representative who leads with partisan messaging or cultural positioning without concrete answers to these material questions is missing the actual basis of electoral choice in this district. The 2024 unopposed result should not be mistaken as permission to ignore the immediate economic pressures that shape daily life for the district's demographic majority." }
