          "The demographic gap in the data — the four reported Census categories account for 95.4% of the population, leaving a 4.6% gap — is modest in absolute terms but worth noting in context: in a district this affluent and educated, any demographic group not fully captured in standard Census reporting may include immigrant communities or mixed-race households whose economic and civic concerns deserve specific representation rather than assumption into the majority category. The unopposed 2024 result tells us about Sutton's current incumbency advantage, not about the district's underlying coalition stability or where future electoral pressure might emerge. At $95,026 median income and 33.9% college education, HD-43 is a district where economic security is broadly distributed but not universal — suburban school funding, housing affordability for young professionals, healthcare access, and whether state government is delivering on quality-of-life priorities are the issues that mobilize voting behavior when partisan default weakens. A representative who assumes the unopposed victory represents durable partisan alignment rather than current incumbency is misreading the district's actual composition."
