          { name: "Student Debt & Early-Career Economic Mobility", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 58.4% college-educated rate combined with 22.7-year median age and $55,401 income creates a constituency of college-degree holders with student debt and limited early-career earnings. Student loan burden, job market access for college graduates, and the relationship between credential attainment and actual wage growth are the material concerns of a young, educated workforce. Legislation addressing debt relief, apprenticeship pathways, and wage standards for degree-holding positions in the state directly affects the economic trajectory of a substantial portion of the district." },
          { name: "Unopposed Status as Complacency Risk", tag: "careful", why: "The unopposed 2024 result creates a strategic trap: it is easy to interpret lack of opposition as evidence of safety, when in fact it more likely reflects Republican resource constraints or strategic focus elsewhere. Young, educated renters at modest income are persuadable on economic issues, and a disciplined Republican challenger with specific messaging on housing and debt could rapidly make this seat competitive. The two-year window without opposition should be used to build a legislative record and constituent relationships that would survive an active challenge, not to assume that unopposed status guarantees reelection." },
          { name: "Assuming Demographic Loyalty as Substitute for Delivery", tag: "avoid", why: "College-educated young renters are not inherently Democratic; they are persuadable on the basis of whether government is delivering on the material concerns that affect their lives. An incumbent who campaigns on abstract partisan messaging or assumes that demographic profile alone ensures reelection without demonstrating concrete delivery on housing, debt, and economic mobility will find that assumption tested rapidly if a credible opponent emerges." },
          { name: "Taking the 76% White Plurality for Granted", tag: "avoid", why: "In a college town district, the 76.17% white plurality likely includes significant numbers of first-time voters, recent transplants, and voters without deep partisan roots. This is not a traditional Republican-base constituency despite the demographic category; it is a young, educated, transient community whose partisan affiliation is contingent on present delivery. Any campaign strategy that treats this demographic as a stable Republican pool available for persuasion, or as a Democratic base that requires no active engagement, misreads the underlying political reality of a district where most residents have limited community tenure and make voting decisions on immediate material concerns." }
