          { name: "Homeowner Economic Security & Cost-of-Living Pressures", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 85.5% homeownership, 45.8-year median age, and $81,939 income, KS-HD-47 is a constituency of asset-holding homeowners with rising fixed costs and income that may not be keeping pace. Property tax fairness, healthcare cost management, Medicare sustainability, and rural service delivery are the material governance concerns most likely to move this base. A legislative record that demonstrates concrete progress on these issues — or a candidate who can speak to them with specificity — consolidates what is currently a 66% plurality into a durable governing coalition." },
          { name: "Education & Workforce Development Gap", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 26.7% college-educated rate substantially underperforms state and national benchmarks, creating a constituency with high property stakes but limited exposure to educational credential-building systems. Families with teenagers, young adults seeking skilled trades, and employers seeking technical workers all face real constraints in a district with below-average college attainment. A legislative focus on K-12 funding adequacy, technical training accessibility, and workforce pipeline development speaks to a genuine constituent need and addresses an opposition vulnerability." },
          { name: "Healthcare Cost Mobilization Among Older Homeowners", tag: "careful", why: "The 45.8-year median age and 85.5% homeownership create a constituency entering the years of highest healthcare cost exposure — prescription drugs, insurance premiums, out-of-pocket care — just as incomes may be plateauing. A well-resourced challenger who makes healthcare affordability and rural hospital viability the centerpiece of their campaign could shift 4-6 percentage points among homeowners experiencing genuine cost pressure. This is the opposition's most plausible expansion path in a district that is otherwise solidly Republican." },
          { name: "Treating 66% as a Safe Floor Rather Than an Active Coalition", tag: "avoid", why: "A 66% result is a comfortable majority but not a durable mandate; it reflects alignment between the incumbent and the district's demographic base, not an expression of deep constituent loyalty to specific policies or a belief that alternatives would be worse. Any representative who campaigns this seat as though 66% is secure and consolidates only the existing Republican base — without actively engaging healthcare costs, education investment, and the 5.5% demographic gap — risks allowing an opponent with genuine constituent focus to narrow the margin significantly." },
          { name: "Dismissing the 5.5% Demographic Gap as Too Small to Matter", tag: "avoid", why: "The 5.5% gap between reported demographic categories and 100% likely includes recent immigrant families, mixed-race households, and other residents whose civic integration and constituent service expectations may differ from the district's Anglo homeowner majority. In a district at 66%, the difference between 66% and 60% is mathematically visible in this small but organizing-capable population. Treating this group as invisible or assuming their integration into the Republican base is both a governance failure and a strategic vulnerability." }
