          { name: "Asian American Community Integration and Opportunity", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 17.52% of the district, Asian Americans represent the second-largest demographic group and are substantially overrepresented in professional sectors, business ownership, and higher education—demographics consistent with a $131,361 median household income. This community's electoral behavior reflects economic interests (business environment, education quality, property values) and social concerns (community safety, quality schools, professional advancement opportunity). Specific engagement on professional sector vitality, K-12 and higher education quality, and business-friendly governance is strategically necessary; treating Asian American voters as a generic 'diversity' constituency rather than engaging their specific economic and educational priorities leaves electoral opportunity on the table." },
          { name: "Young Professional and Renter Economic Positioning", tag: "careful", why: "The 30.4% renter rate—above the national average even in an affluent district—combined with the 39.6-year median age suggests a significant cohort of younger professionals, early-career workers, and older adults choosing urban convenience over suburban homeownership. This group's economic concerns differ from homeowner majorities: housing affordability relative to local market rates, access to walkable employment and transit, school quality if they have children, and economic mobility matter more than property tax rates. In a district this affluent, young renters may be underemployed relative to education level or choosing lifestyle tradeoffs; understanding their specific economic constraints and priorities is essential to holding their vote." },
          { name: "Partisan Team-Sport Framing in a Competence-Driven District", tag: "avoid", why: "KS-HD-48 is competitive (53-47) because a substantial minority of college-educated professionals and affluent voters are willing to cross partisan lines based on candidate quality and governance alignment rather than inherited team loyalty. Any candidate who relies primarily on partisan messaging, tribal affiliation, or abstract ideology without demonstrating specific competence and policy rigor will alienate the exact voters most likely to swing the seat. This district's 53% Democratic result does not represent a stable blue coalition; it represents college-educated professionals' current assessment that the Democratic candidate better serves their governance expectations—an assessment that remains open to revision." },
          { name: "Treating Racial and Ethnic Diversity as a Democratic Asset Rather Than a Lived Constituency", tag: "avoid", why: "While the 17.52% Asian American population and 4.7% Hispanic population certainly overlap with Democratic performance, reducing engagement with these communities to demographic targeting or generic 'diversity' messaging misses the constituency's actual economic and professional interests. Asian American voters in particular are concentrated in sectors (healthcare, technology, education, professional services) that have specific policy needs around licensing portability, credential recognition, education quality, and professional advancement opportunity. Treating these voters as a block to be activated through identity messaging rather than engaging their specific economic and professional interests leaves a competence-driven candidate unable to appeal directly to their material concerns." }
