          { name: "Treating 66% as a Structural Ceiling Rather Than Floor", tag: "avoid", why: "A 66% Republican margin in a district with 87.54% white, high-income, educated homeowners is not an exceptional result—it is a competent consolidation of the natural Republican base in favorable demographics. Interpreting this result as a comfortable and stable foundation for future elections misses the possibility that specific governance failures, property tax frustration, or education funding inadequacy could mobilize the 34% and narrow the margin substantially in the next cycle. The affluence and education of this constituency make it analytically sophisticated about evaluating incumbent performance; assuming demographic destiny is strategically naive." },
          { name: "Framing Opposition as Illegitimate or External", tag: "avoid", why: "The 34% Democratic share in a district this affluent and educated is not the product of carpetbagging or ideological extremism—it reflects real constituents with material concerns about education, governance, or specific policy disagreement with Republican positioning. Any candidate or incumbent who frames this 34% as illegitimate or unrepresentative of 'real' district values is effectively dismissing one-third of their actual constituents and forfeiting the opportunity to understand and address what is driving their opposition. In a district this educated and engaged, such dismissal is both strategically counterproductive and substantively inaccurate." }
