          { name: "Housing Affordability and Renter-Majority Engagement", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 40.5% renter rate in a district with 35.7-year median age and $58,801 income, housing costs represent a direct material constraint on the constituency that elected Schlingensiepen. Renters at this income level face sustained pressure from rising housing costs that consume disproportionate shares of household budgets and constrain savings, education investment, and economic mobility. Legislative action on housing supply, tenant protections, and affordable development—coupled with consistent messaging about the incumbent's understanding of renter economics—directly reinforces alignment with the district's primary economic concern and differentiates Democratic representation from Republican alternatives that frame housing as a market issue rather than a constituent security issue." },
          { name: "College-Educated, Younger Professional Economic Mobility", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 36.1% college attainment and 35.7-year median age signal a constituency of educated, relatively young professionals for whom economic mobility, student debt management, and workforce development are active, ongoing concerns. At $58,801 income, this is a community with credentials but not yet significant accumulated wealth—people who are likely carrying student loans, facing healthcare costs, and evaluating public investment in education and workforce infrastructure as direct personal priorities. Democratic legislative messaging and action on education funding, workforce development, and student debt policy directly speaks to this constituency's material self-interest and differentiates from Republican positioning that prioritizes tax reduction over public investment." },
          { name: "Growing Hispanic, Black, and Asian Communities (19.6% Combined)", tag: "careful", why: "The combined Hispanic (12.62%), Black (5.82%), and Asian (1.19%) populations represent nearly one-fifth of HD-55 and are growing constituencies in Democratic strongholds across Kansas. This community faces specific economic constraints around wage equity, healthcare access, and barriers to professional advancement that may not be fully visible in district-wide median income figures. Active engagement with these communities—including specific legislative attention to their economic and social priorities—is essential both for maintaining current Democratic margins and for demonstrating that the party's representation extends beyond the white college-educated plurality to the full constituency." },
          { name: "Treating 63% as a Durable, Uncontested Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 63-37 result is a strong majority but not a landslide in a district where nearly two-fifths of voters chose the opposition. In a state as Republican as Kansas, this margin reflects active Democratic mobilization and genuine alignment with a specific constituency rather than inherited partisan dominance. Any incumbent who governs as though 63% represents a safe, uncontested base and allows engagement with the renter-majority, younger-professional, and minority constituencies to become passive risks losing the 10-15% of persuadable voters whose material concerns drive their electoral choice." },
          { name: "Allowing Economic Messaging to Drift Toward Ideological Abstraction", tag: "avoid", why: "The Democratic strength in HD-55 is fundamentally economic, not ideological—rooted in housing affordability, education investment, and professional workforce development that younger, educated, renting households perceive as serving their material interests. Any shift toward abstract ideological positioning on cultural or non-economic issues risks alienating the 15-20% of economically persuadable voters who might otherwise be vulnerable to a Republican challenger who effectively repositions around housing costs and economic opportunity. The seat is held by continuous, concrete engagement with the district's actual priorities, not by partisan identity." }
