          "Engage Hispanic constituency with direct economic messaging, not token acknowledgment: the 25.12% Hispanic population is the second-largest demographic group and includes both renters and small business owners whose economic concerns deserve specific legislative attention on wage policy, education investment, and business access to credit and markets.",
          "Understand what generated the 42% opposition: determine whether it is concentrated among renters frustrated by housing costs and wage stagnation, Hispanic voters perceiving Republican economic policy as hostile to immigrant communities, younger adults evaluating on economic mobility, or a combination — and offer concrete answers to each rather than assuming the current coalition is stable.",
          "Do not treat the 58% as a durable majority: in a district with 49.7% renters, 25.12% Hispanic population, and $55,941 median income, the current Republican margin is held by coalition management, not demographic inevitability — any candidate who consolidates only existing Republican voters and neglects the opposition's material economic concerns risks finding HD-60 competitive in the next cycle."
