          "Lead with housing affordability and renter experience: at 47.6% renters in a district with $83,620 income and 59.5% college education, a substantial constituency is navigating housing costs that may be rising faster than their income growth. Zoning, rental regulation, housing supply, and first-time homebuyer access are concrete policy issues with direct constituent impact in a district this educated and this renter-heavy.",
          "Build economic security messaging for professional-class households: college-educated, $83k+ income adults in their mid-30s are evaluating whether their education investment is paying off, whether their income is keeping pace with cost of living, and whether government is delivering on opportunity. This is not a base-mobilization constituency — it is a persuadable middle that responds to specifics about tax fairness, childcare access, and economic mobility.",
          "Do not assume inherited Republican loyalty: a 52% margin in a district this educated, affluent, and young is not a stable coalition — it is a working plurality. The district's professional-class majority will split its vote if candidates offer genuinely different visions of economic fairness and opportunity, and a 48% opposition in a district this blue-collar-light is a warning signal, not background noise."
