          { name: "Upper-Middle-Class Economic Security & Healthcare Costs", tag: "lean-into", why: "The $78,913 median household income places HD-73 in the upper-middle-class range where healthcare costs, retirement security, and property tax management are the primary economic concerns. At 37.5 median age with only 25.7% renters, this is a constituency of established adults managing mortgages, family healthcare, and long-term financial planning. Any candidate who can articulate specific policy responses to healthcare cost inflation, tax fairness for homeowners, and retirement security is addressing the material economic concerns of the district's electoral base." },
          { name: "Hispanic & Black Community Integration", tag: "careful", why: "The combined Hispanic (5.89%) and Black (2.03%) populations represent less than 8% of HD-73 currently, but in a district with unopposed Republican results they are easy to neglect. These communities' integration into the professional and homeowner economy — their access to good-paying jobs, housing, and healthcare — is a forward-looking governance question that will matter if they grow or if a future challenger mobilizes them. Specific constituent attention now, before these communities become a contested swing factor, is more efficient than reactive outreach after they have organized around grievances." },
          { name: "Treating Unopposed Status as Evidence of Electoral Safety", tag: "avoid", why: "A 2024 unopposed result in a Republican-lean district should be interpreted as a sign of incumbent strength and Democratic weakness in this cycle, not as evidence that the seat is structurally safe. The demographic profile — college-educated professionals, upper-middle-class income, suburban location — is precisely the swing constituency nationally that has shown capacity to punish incumbents on education, healthcare, and tax governance. The moment a credible challenger emerges with a compelling education or healthcare message, the unopposed margin becomes irrelevant." },
          { name: "Assuming the Professional Majority's Partisan Loyalty is Fixed", tag: "avoid", why: "College-educated professionals and higher-income homeowners have demonstrated nationally and in Kansas that they are willing to cross party lines if they perceive superior governance on issues they care about. Campaigning HD-73 as though the professional majority's Republican loyalty is a solid foundation, rather than as a coalition that must be actively retained through performance on education quality, healthcare affordability, and local economic opportunity, misreads the actual swing dynamics of this demographic." }
