          { name: "Rural Healthcare Access and Service Delivery", tag: "lean-into", why: "A district with 41.2-year median age and stable homeownership is aging in place, and rural healthcare access — hospital viability, specialist availability, and rural EMS — becomes a direct quality-of-life and property-value concern. At $64,442 income, households do not have unlimited resources to travel for care or absorb rising healthcare costs, and the 27% opposition in this stable, prosperous-looking district may include rural households whose access to healthcare has degraded over the incumbent's tenure. Demonstrating legislative action on rural healthcare — whether through Medicaid policy, rural hospital funding, or telehealth infrastructure — is both a governance obligation and a credibility-building opportunity with a constituency that looks Republican but has real, unmet healthcare needs." },
          { name: "Understanding the 27% Opposition in a Safe Seat", tag: "careful", why: "A 73-27 margin is decisive, but 27% of a district actively preferring the opposition — in a state where Republican margins are often substantially larger — represents real dissatisfaction that deserves investigation rather than dismissal. Whether the 27% is concentrated among younger renters evaluating on economic mobility and healthcare access, among rural workers frustrated by agricultural policy outcomes, or among specific geographic pockets dissatisfied with incumbent performance matters for both holding the seat and understanding whether the margin is stable or at risk if a credible challenger emerges. Any candidate treating 73% as an immovable floor without understanding what drives the 27% is missing a strategic opportunity to either hold the seat more comfortably or prepare for a credible challenge." },
          { name: "Treating a 73% Result as Sufficient Mandate Without Engaging Opposition Concerns", tag: "avoid", why: "The 73-27 margin is decisive in the current cycle, but it is not a warrant to ignore the 27% or assume it represents unengageable opposition. In Kansas districts, changing turnout patterns, candidate quality effects, and shifts in specific issue salience can move 5-7 points relatively quickly, and a candidate who campaigns this seat as though 73% guarantees safety without actively engaging the working-class opposition's concerns risks being vulnerable in the next competitive cycle." },
          { name: "Neglecting the ~4% Demographic Gap and Native American Community Engagement", tag: "avoid", why: "While the AIAN population in KS-HD-79 appears smaller than in Oklahoma or South Dakota districts, the ~4.18% gap still represents real people whose governance interests — particularly around healthcare access through tribal facilities, economic development, and tribal sovereignty issues — may not align perfectly with the homeowner-majority agenda. In rural Kansas, Native American communities can organize around specific legislative actions, and even modest shifts in turnout or candidate preference among a 4% population can contribute to narrowing margins over time if that community perceives the incumbent as unresponsive to their needs." }
