          { name: "Economic Opportunity and Wage Growth for Multiracial Working Class", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 20.7% Hispanic/Latino and 15.89% Black communities together represent nearly 37% of HD-83 and are almost certainly the core of Helgerson's 58% winning coalition. These constituencies evaluate representation on whether government is delivering concrete improvements in job access, wage growth, small-business opportunity, and economic mobility for their families — not on abstract ideological positioning. Helgerson's coalition is durable only if legislative action and constituent service consistently demonstrate that government is improving material economic circumstances for these communities; the 42% opposition will argue the opposite, so measurable economic wins are essential to coalition retention." },
          { name: "White Working-Class Coalition Management", tag: "careful", why: "Approximately 30% of HD-83 is white working-class voters at $50,629 income, and a meaningful share of this constituency shifted Democratic to produce the 58% result — a breakage from historical Republican alignment that is persuadable but not durable. These voters are evaluating on whether government is improving their economic security, not on inherited partisan identity; the 42% opposition will aggressively challenge Helgerson by arguing that Democratic governance has worsened affordability or failed to deliver on economic promises. Retention of white working-class voters requires consistent communication about economic improvements and genuine engagement with their specific concerns around housing, healthcare, and job security, not assumption that they will remain loyal to the Democratic coalition." },
          { name: "Positioning Democratic Governance as Failed or Inefficient", tag: "avoid", why: "The 42% Republican opposition in a district this economically stressed is a cohesive coalition that will argue Democratic governance has worsened affordability, created waste, or failed to deliver on promises — a credible argument if Helgerson's legislative record includes policies that can be characterized as expanding government without delivering tangible material improvements. Any incumbent whose record includes higher costs, ineffective programs, or broken promises to this constituency will find the 58% margin collapses as swing voters return to Republican alignment out of economic frustration rather than ideological conviction." },
          { name: "Taking the Multiracial Coalition for Granted", tag: "avoid", why: "The 20.7% Hispanic/Latino and 15.89% Black communities are nearly 37% of the district and form the foundation of the 58% result, but these constituencies are not permanent Democratic voters — they are responsive to whether government is actually improving their economic circumstances. An incumbent who assumes this coalition is locked in and reduces constituent service, legislative attention, or visible engagement with these communities will find turnout and persuasion both deteriorate; in a 58-42 district, even modest declines in minority voter participation or support create a Republican opening." }
