          "Lead on housing affordability and renter protections: at 48.4% renters and $50,629 income, nearly half the district is directly vulnerable to housing cost shocks — tenant protections, affordable housing development, and rent regulation are not ideological issues here, they are survival economics that directly determine whether Helgerson's coalition stays engaged.",
          "Translate the 58% into tangible economic wins: the 42% opposition will argue that Democratic governance has failed to improve affordability or create opportunity — Helgerson's defense must include specific, measurable improvements in rental costs, healthcare access, wage growth, or job creation that voters in this district can see and experience, not abstract policy victories.",
          "Understand and hold the white working-class voters who broke for Helgerson: roughly 30% of the district is white working-class at $50,629 income — these voters are persuadable but not loyal, and they evaluate on whether government is delivering for them economically; the Republican challenge will be to convince them that Helgerson has failed, so consistent communication about economic improvements is essential to retaining this swing constituency."
