          { name: "Renter Constituency & Housing Affordability", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 40.3% renter rate is unusually high for a 58% Republican district and signals the presence of younger adults, service-sector workers, and households priced out of the ownership market despite the district's overall affluence. At median age 36.3 and with significant college attainment, this renter population likely includes young professionals and families struggling with housing costs relative to local incomes — a constituency that tends to evaluate on housing policy, cost-of-living concerns, and whether government is addressing practical affordability. This 40% renter minority is a swing constituency that a Republican incumbent can lose if housing costs continue rising or if an opposition candidate specifically addresses affordability concerns the incumbent treats as peripheral." },
          { name: "42% Opposition in a Prosperous District", tag: "careful", why: "A 58-42 Republican result in a district with $93,439 income and 55.5% college attainment is narrower than these demographic indicators typically produce — suggesting that a meaningful segment of the college-educated, higher-income constituency is either voting Democratic or abstaining from Republican support. This opposition may reflect college-educated voters' concerns about specific Republican positions on education, healthcare, or social issues, or it may signal dissatisfaction with the incumbent's specific performance rather than partisan realignment. Understanding whether the 42% represents ideological Democratic voters, pragmatic swing voters, or disengaged Republicans is essential for determining whether the seat is genuinely competitive or simply contains normal Democratic minority in a safely Republican district." },
          { name: "Tribal Sovereignty & Native American Governance", tag: "avoid", why: "KS-HD-85 does not have a significant Native American population — the reported racial categories account for 98.1% of the population, leaving only ~1.9% unaccounted for — meaning tribal government engagement, Indian Health Service issues, and tribal sovereignty concerns are not substantive legislative priorities for this district's constituents. A candidate who leads with tribal engagement messaging or frames indigenous policy as a core district issue is misreading the district's actual composition and will appear out of touch with the community's real concerns." },
          { name: "Framing as a Working-Class Rural Seat", tag: "avoid", why: "KS-HD-85's median income of $93,439 places it in the upper-middle class range; its 55.5% college attainment and suburban or exurban character (implied by the high income and moderate renter rate) make this a professional-class, educated constituency, not a working-class or rural community. Any candidate who frames this district as a struggling rural area needing populist economic messaging is fundamentally mischaracterizing the district's actual demographic and economic profile, and voters who live in a prosperous, college-educated community will recognize that framing as inauthentic." }
