          { name: "Hispanic Community Economic Opportunity & Workplace Protections", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 23.99% Hispanic population represents nearly one-quarter of HD-86 and is concentrated in the working-class and renter portions of the district, where wage stagnation, workplace exploitation, and immigration enforcement directly affect economic security. Specific legislative attention to wage theft recovery, workplace safety standards, and immigration enforcement patterns that protect working families demonstrates active engagement rather than passive assumption of support. A representative who can point to measurable progress on workplace protections or wage standards for the Hispanic community builds durable political alignment; one who defaults to generic appeals finds this constituency available to a challenger with credible workplace advocacy." },
          { name: "Treating 2024 Unopposed Victory as Evidence of Electoral Safety", tag: "careful", why: "An unopposed 100% victory reflects the strategic absence of organized opposition, not the absence of competitive terrain — a critical distinction in a district this demographically vulnerable to populist or working-class-focused messaging. The 50.9% renter rate, $48,745 income, 24% Hispanic population, and 14.9% college attainment describe a constituency for whom housing costs, wage growth, and economic mobility are primary evaluation criteria, not inherited partisan loyalty. A well-organized challenger who leads on housing affordability, wage standards, or immigration enforcement would likely find HD-86 genuinely competitive; the 2024 result proves nothing about 2026." },
          { name: "Ignoring the Low College-Attainment Signal", tag: "avoid", why: "The 14.9% college-attainment rate is a direct signal of limited access to wage-mobility pathways and higher-income careers, which correlates with both economic anxiety and receptivity to anti-establishment messaging from candidates positioned as challenging the status quo. A representative who addresses education and workforce development as pathways to economic opportunity — rather than abstractly, but with specific investment in community colleges, apprenticeships, and working-class wage growth — builds legitimacy with this constituency; one who ignores the education-access issue leaves it available to a challenger offering alternative solutions." },
          { name: "Relying on Demographic Alignment Without Active Constituent Engagement", tag: "avoid", why: "Even in a district with 24% Hispanic population and 50.9% renters — both demographically aligned with Democratic support — the incumbent cannot assume political durability without active, visible engagement on the material concerns these constituencies face. Renter households need evidence that their representative is working on housing costs; Hispanic constituents need evidence of specific workplace advocacy; younger adults need evidence of economic opportunity. Absence of active constituent service, or assumption that demographic alignment guarantees electoral support, leaves the seat vulnerable to a challenger with genuine credibility on these material issues." }
