        issues: [
          { name: "Homeowner Economic Stability & Property Tax Management", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 72.8% homeownership and $72,750 median household income, HD-97 is a property-holding constituency whose primary state-government leverage is through property tax policy and school funding stability. The 61% result reflects homeowner confidence in Hoheisel's baseline competence on these issues. Any Republican representative who does not demonstrate fluency on tax fairness, school district funding, and the protection of household equity through stable housing policy will create an opening for Democratic challengers to compete for homeowner swing voters who can be persuaded their interests are better served elsewhere." },
          { name: "Hispanic Population Growth & Economic Integration", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 19.51% Hispanic population is nearly five times the Black and Asian communities combined and represents the most significant demographic growth vector in the district. Currently, this population is not translating to Democratic strength — the 61-39 result suggests Hispanic support is split or trending Republican — but this is not a stable baseline. Hispanic voters' evaluation of candidate positioning on immigration, economic opportunity, healthcare access, and community safety is active and contestable. A Republican who builds authentic constituent relationships with Hispanic communities through Spanish-language outreach, specific economic development attention, and genuine policy engagement is consolidating a constituency that could alternatively swing Democratic; a Republican who neglects this community is creating vulnerability." },
          { name: "Renter Constituency Mobilization & Economic Precarity", tag: "careful", why: "At 27.3% of the population, renters represent roughly one-quarter of HD-97's constituency — large enough to swing the seat if fully mobilized but currently underrepresented in electoral outcomes. Renters in a district with this homeownership concentration are systematically younger, more precarious economically, and responsive to different issues (housing costs, eviction protection, wages) than homeowners. If Democratic challengers effectively mobilize renters around housing affordability while the Republican incumbent appears indifferent to their economic concerns, the 61% margin could compress significantly. Hoheisel must demonstrate responsive engagement with renter concerns to prevent this constituency from becoming an organized opposition base." },
          { name: "Ideological or Social-Issue Polarization as Primary Campaign Messaging", tag: "avoid", why: "HD-97's 61-39 Republican margin is built on homeowner satisfaction with baseline economic governance, not on ideological enthusiasm or social-issue alignment. A Republican campaign that emphasizes cultural grievances, abortion positioning, or partisan conflict over school choice or tax policy risks activating the 39% opposition on issues where they have stronger ground than on economic management. In a district where the swing voters are homeowners evaluating on property-tax competence and Hispanic voters evaluating on economic opportunity, ideological positioning that divides rather than consolidates this coalition is strategically self-defeating." },
          { name: "Treating the 39% Opposition as Demographically Locked or Irrelevant", tag: "avoid", why: "The 39% Democratic vote in a 61% Republican district cannot be assumed to be a stable floor or an irrelevant noise. With 19.51% Hispanic population, 27.3% renters, and 34.2-year median age — all demographics that skew younger and less property-rooted than the homeowner base — the opposition contains constituencies that are both growing and responsive to candidate-specific messaging. Any incumbent who campaigns this seat as though 61% is a durable majority rather than a current alignment is vulnerable to a challenger who directly engages the renter and Hispanic constituencies with specific economic proposals that the incumbent has neglected." }
        ],
        memoHeadline: "KS-HD-97: A 61-39 Republican Seat Held by Demographic Stability and Economic Baseline Competence, Where Hispanic Growth and Renter Volatility Present Both Risk and Opportunity",
        memoParagraphs: [
          "KS-HD-97 is a genuinely safe Republican district — Hoheisel's 61-39 margin in 2024 reflects stable alignment between a constituency's material interests and Republican positioning. The district's demographic foundation is straightforward: 64.69% white, 72.8% homeownership (inverse of 27.2% renters), $72,750 median household income, and 26.1% college attainment. This is a middle-class, homeowner-dominant, moderately educated constituency anchored to property values, tax policy, and the stability that comes from owning rather than renting. The 34.2-year median age signals neither a retirement community nor a youth-driven district — it is a working-family constituency at the age where household formation, mortgage obligations, and school-district quality drive voting behavior. At this income level, with this homeownership concentration, the 61-39 result is the natural equilibrium. What holds this seat for Republicans is not passionate ideological alignment but baseline competence on the issues that matter to homeowners: property tax management, school funding stability, and economic conditions that support household finances.",
          "The strategic vulnerability in HD-97 is not the current margin but the composition of the opposition and the direction of demographic change. The 19.51% Hispanic population is nearly five times the Black (5.29%) and Asian (4.31%) communities, and the gap between reported categories (64.69 + 19.51 + 5.29 + 4.31 = 93.8%) suggests roughly 6.2% American Indian / Alaska Native residents — a smaller but present constituency. More importantly, the 27.3% renter rate in a district where homeownership is the dominant electoral reality means that roughly one-quarter of the constituency operates under entirely different economic logic: renters do not benefit from property tax breaks, do not build household equity, and are more mobile and less rooted to specific communities. Hispanic population growth, combined with renter concentration that skews younger and more economically precarious than the homeowner base, creates a long-term demographic trajectory where a 61-39 seat could compress toward 58-42 or worse over two to three election cycles if the Republican position on housing affordability, renter protection, and Hispanic economic opportunity appears indifferent or hostile. Hoheisel's 61% holds because the homeowner majority is satisfied with baseline governance; any challenger who mobilizes the renter constituency and Hispanic community around concrete economic grievances while Hoheisel takes them for granted has a viable path to competitive positioning."
        ],
        memoBullets: [
          "Hold the homeowner base through competent property tax and school funding governance — the 72.8% homeownership rate is the numerical foundation of the 61% result, and any erosion in confidence on these issues directly threatens the margin.",
          "Engage Hispanic constituents and the renter population on housing affordability, renter protections, and pathways to homeownership; the 19.51% Hispanic population and 27.3% renter rate together represent a growing share of the district that operates on different economic logic than the homeowner majority and requires active constituent attention rather than assumption of stability.",
          "Address economic competence directly: at $72,750 income, this constituency evaluates on whether government is delivering stable, predictable conditions for household finances — property tax fairness, school quality, jobs availability — not ideological positioning or social issues.",
          "Monitor renter turnover and Hispanic voter participation as early warning signals; a 61-39 seat becomes competitive when new renters move in faster than the homeowner base can absorb them politically, or when Hispanic voter mobilization increases from current baseline levels."
        ]
