          { name: "Educational Investment and Workforce Pipeline", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 37.8% college attainment, KS-HD-100 has above-average educational attainment but also reflects a constituency where post-secondary training and workforce preparation are economically consequential. The combination of strong median household income and modest (not exceptional) college rates suggests that technical training, community college funding, and pathways from education to skilled employment matter materially to households' economic mobility. School funding adequacy, vocational training access, and partnerships between secondary education and local employers are the legislative priorities that demonstrate engagement with the district's actual economic composition." },
          { name: "Demographic Gap Constituency Identification and Engagement", tag: "careful", why: "The four reported Census categories account for only 90.62% of the district's population, leaving a 9.38% gap that — depending on district geography and composition — may represent American Indian / Alaska Native residents, recent immigrant communities, or both. In a 61-39 Republican district, this constituency of 1,200-1,400 voters is small enough that erosion of support from this group alone cannot flip the seat, but large enough that their economic and civic concerns deserve genuine representation rather than assumption or neglect. Understanding whether this gap represents a latent constituency that current representation is missing is both a governance obligation and a latent strategic insight." },
          { name: "Treating 61% as a Structural Majority Rather Than an Active Result", tag: "avoid", why: "A 61-39 Republican result in Kansas suggests stability, but does not guarantee it if the incumbent assumes that the partisan composition is durable without active engagement across the district's actual population. The 25.6% renter rate and 9.38% demographic gap together represent approximately 11,000-12,000 constituents whose material concerns and electoral behavior are not fully reflected in the partisan margin. Any candidate who consolidates only the homeowner base and assumes renters and demographic-gap constituencies will remain politically inactive risks allowing an alternative candidate to mobilize them effectively." },
          { name: "Partisan Ideology as a Substitute for Constituent Service", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district with $86,607 median income and 37.8% college attainment, constituents are economically engaged and informed about policy specifics. An incumbent or challenger who campaigns on abstract Republican positioning rather than concrete answers to the material concerns that drive renter household and younger adult constituencies — housing affordability, childcare costs, wage progression, school funding adequacy — will find that the 39% opposition is more resilient than a 61-39 margin might suggest. This constituency evaluates on whether government is delivering for them, not on partisan label alone." }
