          { name: "Assuming 65% Represents a Durable Safe Seat", tag: "avoid", why: "In deeply red Kansas, 65% is not a large margin — it is a comfortable but not dominant result that, in a district this demographically favorable, represents underperformance relative to partisan baseline. Any incumbent or challenger who campaigns this seat as though 65% is a floor rather than the current equilibrium between a favorable base and unmet constituent concerns is failing to recognize the vulnerability that could emerge if economic conditions shift or a credible challenger emerges with specific constituent service record." },
          { name: "Treating the Renter Minority (25.4%) as Politically Irrelevant", tag: "avoid", why: "While homeowners dominate HD-104, 25.4% renter rate represents approximately one-quarter of the district's households — potentially younger adults, service-sector workers, or others with material economic concerns distinct from homeowner priorities (housing affordability, local wage floors, service-sector job quality). A representative who campaigns exclusively to homeowner concerns and leaves the renter constituency unengaged is ceding a meaningful constituency to any challenger who offers specific economic messaging to renters, particularly if economic conditions tighten in the next election cycle." }
