          "The unopposed 2024 victory conceals the strategic reality: the 3.23% American Indian/Alaska Native population gap and the absence of any serious Republican challenge in the last cycle means there has been no active measurement of how much of the district's Democratic lean is genuine coalition-building versus default partisan alignment in a blue-trending area. The 50% college-educated population and 36.3 median age suggest a constituency that votes on policy substance and personal economic impact rather than inherited party loyalty. Woodard's path to making this seat genuinely durable — rather than simply safe until a credible challenger emerges — requires active engagement around the housing affordability crisis that confronts renters in an expensive market, tenant protections and fair-lease frameworks, the cost of childcare and education for young professional households, and the material conditions that determine whether a college-educated, renting professional in Kansas stays or leaves. An incumbent who consolidates Democratic margins without addressing these substantive concerns risks finding that the 100% margin in an unopposed race does not translate to durability in a genuinely contested election."
