          { name: "Renter & Young Professional Economic Security", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 40.3% renter rate is notably high for a Kansas state house district and reflects a constituency with material housing cost pressures and less asset stability than homeowner-majority districts. Combined with the 31.4-year median age, this identifies a population with direct economic sensitivity to wage stagnation, housing costs, and healthcare expenses — the precise issues Democratic challengers target in swing districts. Proactive attention to housing affordability, cost-of-living pressures, and whether wages are keeping pace with regional economic growth is the mechanism to consolidate this constituency and prevent them from becoming a swing group in a competitive cycle." },
          { name: "The Electoral Durability of the 73% Margin", tag: "careful", why: "A 73% result in a district with 41.6% college-educated adults, 40.3% renters, and a 31.4-year median age does not reflect demographic inevitability; it reflects either exceptional candidate quality, Democratic organizational absence, or both. In comparable Kansas and Colorado districts over the past two cycles, 70%+ Republican margins have eroded when Democratic challengers developed specific economic messaging for college-educated younger voters. The current margin is real but not structurally durable — any incumbent strategy that assumes the 73% as a floor rather than a snapshot of current conditions is arithmetically underestimating the district's actual competitive potential." },
          { name: "Treating College-Educated Voters as a Locked Republican Constituency", tag: "avoid", why: "The 41.6% college-educated population in HD-111 is not an inherently Republican constituency; in the contemporary political environment, college-educated professionals evaluate representatives on education funding, healthcare access, and whether policy serves their economic interests. Assuming this group as secure Republican voters — or engaging them only on abstract conservative positioning rather than on concrete policy delivery — creates vulnerability to a challenger who develops specific proposals on education affordability and healthcare that resonate with this demographic." },
          { name: "Overlooking the Renter Constituency as Politically Marginal", tag: "avoid", why: "The 40.3% renter rate makes renters a substantial constituency in HD-111, not a marginal group, and their economic concerns — housing costs, wage growth, healthcare access — are distinct from homeowner-majority concerns. Any candidate strategy that treats the renter population as politically negligible or secondary to homeowner concerns is strategically blind to a population that represents two-fifths of the district and evaluates representatives on whether their material economic pressures are being addressed." }
