          { name: "Hispanic Community Economic Integration", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 17.83% Hispanic population in a district where Brantley won 71-29, the Hispanic community represents a substantial constituency whose economic concerns — workforce participation, housing access, and service-sector job quality — are material to household stability in a $57,138 median-income district. The 35.4% renter rate suggests significant housing cost pressure, particularly acute for working Hispanic families in southwestern Kansas. A candidate who engages this community with specificity on economic mobility and housing affordability builds both durable coalition support and demonstrates responsiveness to the district's actual demographic composition rather than its partisan margin." },
          { name: "Rural Working-Class Economic Security", tag: "lean-into", why: "With a median income of $57,138, a college attainment of only 22.8%, and a 38.6-year median age, HD-112 is a rural working-class constituency whose economic foundation rests on agriculture, food processing, and service employment — sectors facing real cost pressures and supply-chain volatility. At this income level and educational attainment, households are particularly exposed to healthcare costs, property tax increases, and utility rate changes that consume a larger share of household budgets. A legislative agenda anchored in cost-of-living relief and workforce stability speaks directly to material constituent concerns rather than abstract ideology." },
          { name: "Hispanic Voter Turnout and Representation", tag: "careful", why: "The 71-29 partisan margin in a district with 17.83% Hispanic population warrants examination of whether this margin reflects low Hispanic turnout, Hispanic voter support for the Republican incumbent, or some combination — each interpretation carries different strategic implications for future cycles. If Hispanic turnout is suppressed relative to the community's population share, then mobilization around economic and community concerns could meaningfully shift both turnout and margins. If Hispanic voters are supporting the incumbent, understanding why — whether through personal relationship-building, specific legislative service, or other factors — is essential to competitive positioning." },
          { name: "Treating the 71% Margin as Durable Without Active Constituent Engagement", tag: "avoid", why: "A 71% result in a district with 17.83% Hispanic population, 35.4% renters, and median income of $57,138 should not be mistaken for a safe, passive hold. In rural southwest Kansas, where demographic change is steady and economic pressures on working-class households are real, a large partisan margin can mask underlying constituent dissatisfaction that mobilizes suddenly when opposition campaigns with authentic economic solutions. Brantley's 71% is a current result, not a demographic guarantee." },
          { name: "Dismissing the District's Limited College Attainment as Irrelevant to Education Policy", tag: "avoid", why: "At 22.8% college attainment in a rural district with limited four-year university infrastructure, families' education decisions are constrained by geography and cost — vocational training, community college pathways, and high school workforce preparation are the education issues that matter materially to most households. A legislative position that treats college attainment as the primary education metric, or that addresses education primarily through higher-education funding debates, misses the actual educational and workforce development needs of a 77.2% non-college-educated constituency whose children and grandchildren need viable alternatives to four-year degree pathways." }
