          { name: "College-Educated Suburban Voter Consolidation", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 55.4% college attainment and $125,382 median income, KS-HD-117 is a highly educated affluent district where Republicans should be winning at 65-70%, not 58%. College-educated suburban voters — particularly women and professionals in knowledge-economy sectors — have demonstrated increasing electoral flexibility in recent cycles, particularly on education funding, healthcare access, and institutional stability. Consolidating this demographic around a positive vision of education investment and community competence is the single most important path to moving beyond a 58% margin and turning this district into a stable Republican hold." },
          { name: "Local Community Quality-of-Life Governance", tag: "lean-into", why: "With a median age of 38.3, 83.6% homeownership, and income at $125,382, this is a suburban constituency whose direct economic interests revolve around school district viability, property values, and local service delivery quality. A legislative agenda rooted in education funding adequacy, infrastructure maintenance, and efficient local government — rather than statewide culture-war positioning — speaks directly to what affluent suburban homeowners actually care about. This is where Republican messaging can outflank Democratic opposition by demonstrating governance competence on the issues that matter most to the constituency's real economic life." },
          { name: "Understanding the 42% Opposition in an Affluent, Educated District", tag: "careful", why: "A 42% opposition result in a district with this income and education profile is unusually high and reflects real voter preference, not measurement error. The opposition is concentrated among college-educated voters, younger professionals, and affluent families who are not voting Republican reflexively — they are voting Democratic based on specific policy and governance concerns. Treating this 42% as immovable core opposition, rather than as persuadable swing voters on education and healthcare policy, is strategically incorrect and leaves winnable voters on the table." },
          { name: "Assuming High Income Equals Automatic Republican Voting", tag: "avoid", why: "The most dangerous assumption in KS-HD-117 is that a $125,382 median income and 55.4% college attainment automatically generate Republican support. College-educated affluent voters in suburban districts have demonstrated they will vote Democratic or persuadable on specific governance and education issues. Any candidate who campaigns this seat by taking the affluent base for granted and focusing instead on turnout-driving culture-war messaging is abandoning the constituency's actual economic and governance concerns and leaving the seat vulnerable to a challenger who does engage them." },
          { name: "Messaging That Positions Education as a Culture-War Issue Rather Than a Community Asset", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district where 55.4% of adults have college education and median income is $125,382, the constituency has a direct material stake in education quality and funding adequacy. Messaging that treats education as a battleground for culture-war positioning rather than as a shared community investment alienates the most educated and affluent voters in the district — precisely the voters who should be the Republican base but who are currently producing only a 58% margin. This messaging choice actively suppresses Republican performance among the demographic that should be delivering a 65%+ margin." }
