          { name: "Treating the District as Homogeneously Conservative", tag: "avoid", why: "The 87.26% white, 25.6% college-educated profile may encourage a legislator to default to social conservative or anti-government positioning without testing whether that actually aligns with constituent economic priorities. At $65,351 income and 39.9 median age, households are concerned with whether government delivers results on healthcare, housing affordability, and job training — not ideological purity. Rural and working-class constituencies with real economic pressure often support government investment when it directly improves their material conditions, regardless of partisan framing. A legislator who assumes that white, non-college-educated means automatically receptive to anti-government messaging misses the opportunity to build durable support by demonstrating that their legislative work improves household economic stability." }
