          { name: "Fiscal Responsibility & Competent Governance", tag: "lean-into", why: "In a district with $136,635 median income and 86% homeownership, affluent professionals evaluate state representatives on whether they deliver efficient, competent management of public resources — particularly schools, infrastructure, and property tax policy. The 55-45 margin in a district this prosperous is narrow enough to indicate that a meaningful share of high-income, college-educated voters are either voting based on governance competence rather than party, or actively choosing opposition. A representative who can point to specific legislative achievements in education funding, transportation infrastructure, and fiscal discipline builds durable support; one who relies on partisan positioning without demonstrating results creates opportunity for a credible challenger." },
          { name: "School Funding & Educational Investment", tag: "lean-into", why: "A 48.1% college-educated constituency with median age 37.8 — established professionals and families with school-age children — has direct material stakes in education funding quality and whether schools are delivering competitive educational outcomes. School funding adequacy, teacher compensation, and curriculum competitiveness are concrete policy concerns for this demographic, and they evaluate representatives based on whether legislative records reflect genuine commitment to educational investment rather than abstract budget-cutting rhetoric. In suburban districts nationally, education funding has become a primary dividing line between constituencies motivated by competent service delivery and those driven by ideological priorities." },
          { name: "Minority Community Visibility & Service Access", tag: "careful", why: "The 9.39% Hispanic and 5.51% Black populations in a district that is 75.69% white and deeply affluent can become invisible to political representation even though their constituent needs around housing stability, workforce development, and healthcare access are material and present. In a district this prosperous, minority communities may include service-sector workers, construction and agricultural workers, and recent immigrants whose economic concerns are different from the Anglo professional majority. A representative who addresses minority community needs with specific attention rather than assuming they are absorbed into a broader coalition demonstrates that representation is inclusive and builds durable support across community lines." },
          { name: "Partisan Cultural Warfare as a Primary Message", tag: "avoid", why: "A 48.1% college-educated, $136,635-income suburban constituency is significantly less motivated by partisan cultural messaging than by questions of governance competence and whether state government is delivering concrete results. Affluent professionals in high-education districts are increasingly persuadable to vote based on institutional stability and good governance rather than on cultural grievance or ideological purity. A representative who campaigns primarily on cultural issues rather than demonstrating competent management of the district's material concerns — education, infrastructure, fiscal responsibility — creates an opening for a challenger to position themselves as the competence alternative." },
          { name: "Taking the 86% Homeowner Base for Granted", tag: "avoid", why: "In prosperous suburban districts nationally, homeowners have shifted from prioritizing simple tax-cutting to valuing community investment — schools, parks, transportation, services — which often requires adequate public funding and effective local governance. A representative who seems primarily interested in reducing public investment rather than ensuring that tax dollars are spent wisely on things homeowners actually want creates vulnerability among the district's largest demographic group. At 55-45, assuming the homeowner base is a static floor without actively demonstrating responsiveness to their evolving priorities is a recipe for losing a seat this close." }
