          { name: "Affluent Homeowner Stability & Service Delivery", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 69.1% homeownership, a 39.1-year median age, and $97,116 income, KS-SD-7 is a stable, affluent, asset-holding community whose economic concerns center on property values, school quality, and municipal service delivery rather than economic survival. Property tax fairness, infrastructure investment, and the quality of local governance are the practical issues that motivate this constituency. The Democratic 64% result reflects satisfaction with the current governance approach; maintaining that margin requires continuous demonstration that investment in the district's schools, parks, roads, and services is producing visible returns." },
          { name: "Hispanic/Latino Community Integration", tag: "careful", why: "At 7.17% of the district, the Hispanic/Latino community is small enough that its specific needs can become subsumed into broader demographic narratives, yet large enough (roughly 2,400-2,800 residents in a typical state senate district) that targeted attention and specific policy responsiveness matter to turnout and support. In an affluent, college-educated, predominantly white district, Hispanic families — whether in service industries, healthcare, or professional sectors — deserve explicit constituent engagement rather than being treated as a secondary demographic. The difference between 7.17% passive inclusion and 7.17% active mobilization can move margins in a district this unified." },
          { name: "Treating 64% as a Durable Supermajority Without Monitoring Opposition", tag: "avoid", why: "A 64-36 margin in a state senate district where Democrats hold the White House and control the state legislature can feel stable, but it is not guaranteed. In affluent suburban districts, single-issue mobilization (property taxes, school curriculum, zoning) can activate the 36% opposition quickly if they perceive governance failure. The incumbent who assumes the current margin is structural rather than earned through continuous constituent service and responsiveness to the affluent homeowner and college-educated professional constituency is vulnerable to a well-organized challenger focused on a specific grievance." },
          { name: "Overlooking the 36% Republican Base's Actual Concerns", tag: "avoid", why: "The 36% Republican result in an affluent, college-educated district is not a monolith of ideological conservatives — it likely includes fiscal conservatives concerned about tax levels and government spending, business owners focused on regulatory environment, and homeowners skeptical of particular policy initiatives (transit, density, environmental regulation). Dismissing this 36% as MAGA-adjacent or ideologically hostile rather than engaging their actual material concerns (property tax burden, business environment, school choice) leaves room for a Republican challenger to build a credible counternarrative around fiscal responsibility and limited government that resonates with affluent households concerned about their tax burden." }
