          { name: "Healthcare Cost & Affordability", tag: "lean-into", why: "Even in a $115,443 median income district, healthcare costs are the primary financial stressor for affluent households — and college-educated voters are responsive to candidates who demonstrate understanding of insurance market mechanics, pharmaceutical cost drivers, and the relationship between state policy and healthcare accessibility. This is a policy area where Republican and Democratic approaches can both appeal to this constituency if grounded in evidence rather than ideology; demonstrating specific knowledge of Kansas healthcare challenges is a credibility marker." },
          { name: "The 48% Opposition & Educated Voter Persuasion", tag: "careful", why: "The 48% Democratic result in a district this affluent and educated is not a stable opposition base — it is a persuadable constituency of college-educated swing voters who are responsive to substantive policy engagement and punish partisan extremism. Understanding what specifically drove their 2024 preference — fiscal concerns, healthcare access, institutional governance, education policy — is essential, because this constituency is capable of moving if offered a credible alternative; treating the 48% as a partisan bloc to be ignored rather than engaged is the single most likely error in this district." },
          { name: "Partisan Ideological Positioning as Governing Strategy", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district where 56.2% of adults hold college degrees and median household income is $115,443, campaigns that rely on partisan culture-war framing or abstract ideological rhetoric will underperform relative to the district's actual political alignment. College-educated affluent voters evaluate on demonstrated competence, policy literacy, and institutional responsibility; they are alienated by candidates who substitute ideology for evidence-based governance reasoning." },
          { name: "Assuming the 52% Margin is Durable Without Engagement", tag: "avoid", why: "The 52-48 result in a district this educated and affluent is not a floor — it is a conditional margin held by demonstration of competent, fiscally responsible governance. Any incumbent or challenger who campaigns as though 52% provides permission to govern without substantive attention to the educated constituency's specific policy concerns will find that margin contract significantly; treating the opposition as a permanent minority to be managed rather than as persuadable educated voters to be engaged is a strategic error in KS-SD-10." }
