          { name: "Demographic Retention & Economic Opportunity for Young Adults", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 21.2% college attainment and 25.6% renter rate signal a district struggling to retain young adults, and the 41.1 median age reflects a population that will naturally age out without replacement. Out-migration of educated young adults to stronger job markets is a slow-acting threat that erodes future Republican margins. Broadband infrastructure, small-business support, and workforce development that creates local pathways for career building are investments that preserve the district's electoral foundation by keeping future voters in the district." },
          { name: "Understanding the 9% Demographic Gap & Native American Engagement", tag: "careful", why: "The four reported Census categories account for only 91% of the population, leaving a 9% gap that — in rural Kansas — most likely represents American Indian / Alaska Native residents. The current 73-27 margin does not reveal whether this population is aligned with or opposed to the incumbent, but in rural districts with Native American communities, voter mobilization around tribal sovereignty, healthcare access, and economic development has demonstrated capacity to shift competitive races. Authentic engagement with the district's Native American community is both a governance obligation and an electoral risk-mitigation strategy." },
          { name: "Treating 73% as a Durable Supermajority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 73% margin in a district with significant demographic challenges — aging population, college-attainment below 22%, renter rate above 25%, and a 9% unaccounted demographic gap — is not a signal of inherent electoral safety. Demographic change, younger cohort turnout, and Native American voter mobilization around specific issues are all capable of closing this margin in future cycles. Any incumbent who governs as though 73% represents a permanent floor rather than a result requiring active maintenance will find the margin eroded by shifting constituency composition." },
          { name: "Assuming Rural Homeowner Alignment as Guaranteed", tag: "avoid", why: "While older homeowners currently support the incumbent at high levels, this support is conditional on delivery on economic security issues — property-tax fairness, healthcare access, rural service viability. At $54,611 income with rising fixed costs, this constituency's support is vulnerability-prone if economic conditions deteriorate or if the incumbent is perceived as prioritizing other constituencies. Rural homeowner loyalty is earned through visible results, not inherited through party affiliation; failure to deliver on the material concerns that drove 73% support is the most direct path to margin erosion." }
