          { name: "Healthcare Access for Aging Professional Constituency", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 36.4-year median age and 92k income place a significant portion of the district in the 50-70 age range — the cohort for whom healthcare cost, Medicare sustainability, and rural healthcare access become urgent material concerns. The district's 32.8% college attainment and professional workforce means these are households with healthcare expectations shaped by professional employment and the ability to pay; they will evaluate representatives on whether state policy is protecting Medicare, controlling healthcare inflation, and ensuring access to quality care. This is not a young district with deferred healthcare concerns; it is a working-age and early-retirement cohort that expects representation focused on healthcare security." },
          { name: "Demographic Stability & Partisan Consolidation", tag: "careful", why: "KS-SD-26's 71-23 margin and demographic profile (82% white, 33% college-educated, 92k income) reflect a consolidated Republican constituency with no obvious path to significant demographic change or partisan shift in the near term. The 23% Democratic baseline is stable rather than growing, and there is no meaningful Hispanic, Asian, or younger-cohort expansion that would alter the electoral math. A candidate should recognize that in a district this consolidated, the risk is not losing to a demographic trend but losing to complacency or a local governance failure that discredits the representative; treating the 71% as structural rather than contingent leads to the inattention that creates vulnerability." },
          { name: "Treating the 23% Democratic Minority as a Swing Constituency", tag: "avoid", why: "The 23% Democratic result in KS-SD-26 does not represent persuadable swing voters or a growing Democratic coalition; it represents the district's baseline Democratic voters, many of whom are likely concentrated in specific towns or neighborhoods and have stable partisan preference. Attempting to win swing voters by moving toward the center or adopting moderate positions misses the reality that the 71% margin is built on the 82% white, affluent, homeowner-dominant constituency that prefers clear Republican governance. The Democratic 23% is not winnable through compromise positioning; it is simply the opposition baseline." },
          { name: "Positioning Affluent Homeowners as a Special Interest", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district where the median household income is 92k, homeownership is 82%, and the constituency is predominantly white and college-educated professionals, any framing that positions this group as a narrow special interest rather than the primary constituency will be perceived as insulting and inaccurate. The 71% result reflects this group's confidence that the representative prioritizes their interests and reflects their values; a candidate who adopts rhetoric suggesting wealthy homeowners are one interest group among many will undermine the trust that sustains the Republican margin in a district this affluent and consolidated." }
