          { name: "Non-White and Demographic Minority Constituents", tag: "careful", why: "The ~5.8% gap between reported Census categories and 100% — likely American Indian, immigrant, or other minority residents — represents a constituency that is often invisible in districts this heavily white and homeowner-dominant but whose concerns are real and present. Healthcare access, economic opportunity, and fair treatment in local institutions (schools, law enforcement, business licensing) are the material governance issues for these constituents, and they can mobilize politically if they perceive chronic neglect. Treating this gap as a rounding error rather than a constituent community is both poor governance and unnecessary electoral risk in a state with Native American tribal presence." },
          { name: "Partisan Consolidation Without Constituent Service", tag: "avoid", why: "A 73% result can create a false sense of security in which an incumbent assumes the homeowner base is locked in and focuses resources on partisan messaging rather than constituent service. This is a strategic error: a margin this large is actually the result of effective service to the dominant constituency, and allowing that service to atrophy — or giving the 27% opposition the impression that the incumbent takes them for granted — creates an opening for a challenger to mobilize alternative constituencies. The 27% is not a trivial minority, and any incumbent who campaigns as if 73% is an immutable floor rather than a result that must be actively defended is vulnerable." },
          { name: "Dismissing Renter and Youth Economic Concerns", tag: "avoid", why: "With 26.8% of the population renting and a 39.3 median age that includes substantial numbers of younger adults, KS-SD-32 contains constituencies with material concerns about housing affordability, wage stagnation, and economic opportunity that differ fundamentally from homeowner financial security. Any legislative record or campaign message that dismisses these concerns as frivolous or incompatible with conservative principles is actively alienating nearly 27% of the district's population — and is mathematically close to the current opposition total. A 73% margin can contract quickly when younger voters and renters conclude that the incumbent's priorities do not include them." }
