          "Treat the 80-20 result as an incumbent performance baseline to be explained and a challenger opportunity to be exploited: the margin likely reflects either genuine constituent satisfaction or weak opposition engagement, and only specific voter contact will determine which. Any campaign that assumes the margin is durable without understanding its source is strategically vulnerable.",
          "Engage Hispanic and Native American communities with specificity: at 12.29% Hispanic and ~13% demographic gap, these communities represent roughly one in four constituents, yet an 80-20 result offers no visibility into their actual voting behavior or economic priorities. Direct constituent engagement on wage floors, housing access, and economic mobility is essential to avoid miscounting the district's true political composition.",
          "Do not campaign this seat as if it is safe or as if the 20% opposition can be ignored: in a working-class district where cost-of-living pressures are real and immediate, a challenger who invests in voter contact and offers specific answers to household economic concerns can credibly compete even against an 80-20 incumbent, particularly if the incumbent's legislative record is weak on economic security specifics."
