          "Monitor housing costs and renter household economics relentlessly: At 33.2% renters and $68,204 median income, one-third of the district is one housing crisis away from economic instability. If rents rise faster than wages or if housing affordability deteriorates, the Latino renter constituency becomes a swing cohort where Democratic messaging on affordable housing gains traction; this is the vulnerability to watch.",
          "Engage the 19.5% college-educated population as a potential opposition anchor: While the district has low overall college attainment, the college-educated 19.5% are likely concentrated in professional and technical roles and may have different policy preferences (education spending, environmental regulation, abortion access) than the working-class majority. This small cohort may be a reliable Democratic base if mobilized; ignoring them assumes they are not a swing factor.",
